#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382448 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129171 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "307bccb6-036f-51bb-99ce-327aad8dfdbe" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral When the Integrand Contains Only One Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51a89715-7a54-5300-819b-fa080b061464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382450} {:answer/id #uuid "56fb164e-39a3-5e17-815d-ec81eaeb5642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382451} {:answer/id #uuid "a9cb3d91-b5e5-5157-8a43-6dd0b3fbf045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382452} {:answer/id #uuid "8cf0027f-33d6-5dec-9265-dd1640066c6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382453} {:answer/id #uuid "5f806e22-cdeb-5e59-8750-426e910009d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f806e22-cdeb-5e59-8750-426e910009d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382454} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2ffc185-1273-5e29-8ff4-7540cee84426" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c9713fa5-57af-5823-80a4-91a27fbe3a41" :question/math-academy-id "q-81244" :question/problem "Calculate ${∬}_{R}({x}^{2}-y+1)\\,\\text{d}A$,  where $R=[-3,0]\\times [0,4].$" :question/worked-solution "Using the linearity property of double integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}({x}^{2}-y+1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{R}{x}^{2}\\text{ }\\text{d}A-{∬}_{R}y\\text{ }\\text{d}A+{∬}_{R}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the first integral is independent of $y.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}{x}^{2}\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =(4-0){\\int }_{-3}^{0}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]_{-3}^{0} \\\\  & =4(\\frac{{0}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(-3)^{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the second integral is independent of $x.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}y\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =(0-(-3)){\\int }_{0}^{4}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third integral is the double integral of unity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\text{d}A=\\text{Area}(R)=3\\cdot 4=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, combining our results, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}({x}^{2}-y+1)\\,\\text{d}A=36-24+12=24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382462 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129172 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8059a197-e9fb-5306-8eed-62c0cea88f33" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral Using the Additivity Over a Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "424c1725-f6be-557b-8ba1-de6cd2c48a82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382464} {:answer/id #uuid "ad38cb99-0e64-5f96-baca-e7d0549cba5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382465} {:answer/id #uuid "1e089d84-f069-5f2c-83fa-ebc8d830878a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382466} {:answer/id #uuid "c6fc8d95-fed9-543b-84f8-e46cb703d09b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382467} {:answer/id #uuid "9f1aadd1-ba01-5e68-a02a-c2ed4e7c1f65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad38cb99-0e64-5f96-baca-e7d0549cba5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382465} :answer-field/id #uuid "d86e0dfb-0f1e-5397-8c0c-93b6e0bbf141" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "265058e2-39b1-59f9-b955-fd7951910713" :question/math-academy-id "q-81253" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$, where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\, & \\text{if}\\,-1<x<1,\\,0<y<\\pi , \\\\ x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\, & \\text{if}\\,1<x<2,\\,0<y<\\pi ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  $R$ is the domain of $f.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we have $R={R}_{1}\\cup {R}_{2}$, where ${R}_{1}=(-1,1)\\times (0,\\pi )$ and ${R}_{2}=(1,2)\\times (0,\\pi ).$ So, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A= & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =(1-(-1)){\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y+({\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2[\\operatorname{sin}⁡y]_{0}^{\\pi }+[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]_{1}^{2}\\cdot [-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =2(0)+(2-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (-(-1)+1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
